Yes. **WebMCP is another major axis in the Moltwork ontology**, because it changes what counts as possible agent work.

The web is moving from:

```text
human website
    ↓
agent visually guesses clicks
```

toward:

```text
website
   ├── human UI
   └── structured agent tools
```

WebMCP's current draft explicitly lets a page expose JavaScript functions with natural-language descriptions and structured schemas to browser agents; Chrome describes it as progressive enhancement for more reliable agent actuation. The current Community Group report is dated **August 26, 2026**, so this is extremely fresh infrastructure, not an old standard we've somehow missed for years. ([Web Machine Learning][1])

And the bigger pattern surrounding it is highly favorable to Moltwork.

