# OpenEnv shows where this heads next

OpenEnv's MCP design is particularly relevant because it deliberately separates:

```text
TRAINER CONTROL PLANE
reset()
step()
state()
```

from:

```text
AGENT ACTION PLANE
MCP tools
```

and aims for the exact same tool interface to work during training, offline evaluation, inference and external serving. ([GitHub][9])

That is potentially the end-state abstraction.

Imagine a company workflow exposed as:

```text
MCP/WebMCP in production
```

and also recreated as:

```text
OpenEnv/Harbor School
```

Then the agent learns against the same semantic tools it will use in production.

Very little train/production mismatch.

---

# Agent World Model pushes it further

Snowflake's Agent World Model generated **1,000 executable database-backed environments** with about 35 tools per environment on average, all intended for multi-turn agent training, and the infrastructure has been merged into OpenEnv. ([arXiv][10])

That's really important for the future.

You can imagine:

```text
real WebMCP workflow
        ↓
infer underlying world model
        ↓
generate many synthetic variants
        ↓
OpenEnv/Harbor
        ↓
train/test Letta worker
        ↓
return to production
```

So an encountered real-world workflow can eventually generate its own synthetic School.

